    "description": "Many of our eating behaviours are not conscious decisions.\n\nThey are habits.\n\nOver time, routines form around food \u2014 when we eat, what we reach for, and how we respond to stress, boredom, or tiredness. These patterns can become automatic and difficult to change. This is why many people find themselves repeating the same cycles with food, even when they genuinely want to do something different. The encouraging news is that habits are **learned patterns**, and learned patterns can be changed.\nIn this workshop we will explore how eating habits develop and how they can be gradually reshaped in a realistic and sustainable way.\n\n### In this session we will cover\n\n\u2022 How eating habits are formed\n\u2022 Why many behaviours around food become automatic\n\u2022 The role of triggers, routines, and rewards\n\u2022 Why simply trying harder rarely works long term\n\u2022 Practical strategies for creating new, supportive habits\n\n### What you will gain\n\nBy the end of the session you will have a clearer understanding of **how your eating patterns work** and how small changes can begin to shift them.\nYou will leave with practical tools to start building healthier and more supportive routines around food.\n\n### Who this workshop is for\n\n\u2022 People who feel stuck in repeating patterns with food\n\u2022 People who want to change their habits without strict dieting\n\u2022 People interested in the psychology of eating behaviour\n\u2022 Anyone looking for a more sustainable approach to health\n\n### Hosted by\n\n**Gaelle Joseph**\nWeight Harmony Coaching & Therapy\n\n**Philip Perre**\nPersonal trainer specialising in strength training and sustainable lifestyle habits.\nTogether we combine **mindset, behaviour change, and movement** to support long-term wellbeing.\n\n### Format\n\n\u2022 Educational talk\n\u2022 Practical strategies\n\u2022 Opportunity for questions and discussion",
